{"id":1,"explanation":"The nonce check in verify prevents a token from being accepted twice. Removing the check against seen would allow replay attacks. #123 is the related issue.","unsure":""}
{"id":2,"explanation":"Call ensure_key_salt before creating KeyManager. If salt setup fails, startup must stop rather than initialize the manager. ensure_key_salt and KeyManager are the named function and class.","unsure":""}
{"id":3,"explanation":"Before allocating buf, keep topN at or below maxSimpleTopN so the resulting slice stays at or below 1 MiB. topN is the requested number of Chunk elements; maxSimpleTopN is its stated limit.","unsure":""}
{"id":4,"explanation":"When meta.abi_version is absent, this mapping must produce None. Treating an absent version as the current ABI could misinterpret the metadata. Abi::from converts a present version; #1784 is the related issue.","unsure":""}
{"id":5,"explanation":"TLP reads /etc/tlp.conf after files matching /etc/tlp.d/*.conf, so a setting there overrides the value written by this drop-in at /etc/tlp.d/90-power.conf. Those paths identify TLP’s main configuration file and drop-in files.","unsure":""}
{"id":6,"explanation":"These profile-specific settings require TLP version 1.10 or later. _AC, _BAT and _SAV are suffixes identifying the power profiles; the shown set_key call sets CPU_ENERGY_PERF_POLICY_ON_BAT for the battery profile. Leave the _AC setting unset so GNOME’s Performance toggle continues to work.","unsure":""}
{"id":7,"explanation":"On a JsonException, return null so the caller can log a parse warning. Throwing the parse error here would bypass that intended handling.","unsure":""}
{"id":8,"explanation":"When AllowedTools is set, RemoveSkipPermissions must strip --dangerously-skip-permissions from args. That flag bypasses the restrictions requested by --allowedTools. AllowedTools and the two flags name the affected tool setting and command-line options.","unsure":""}
{"id":9,"explanation":"Consult is_definitely_dead_code in rust/xray-core/src/compiler/graph_preamble.rs for the verdict table relevant to thunk_is_dead. The path identifies the source file and the name after the colon identifies the function.","unsure":"The comment does not say where within that function the verdict table appears."}
{"id":10,"explanation":"Replace the hard-coded repos_skipped value with one derived from the domains-to-repos mapping. #874 tracks that work.","unsure":""}
{"id":11,"explanation":"index.regex_query(pattern) uses a linear-time DFA and does not backtrack, which explains its expected performance for regex queries.","unsure":""}
{"id":12,"explanation":"Keep the token-family table schema, including this refresh_tokens table, coordinated with RefreshTokenSqliteBackend._ensure_schema in src/storage/refresh_token_backend.py. The path names the backend file and the name after the colon identifies its schema setup method.","unsure":""}
{"id":13,"explanation":"MAX_BATCH is 500 because the vendor’s batch API accepts no more than 500 items per request. doc:vendor-api#limits points to the limits section of the vendor API documentation.","unsure":""}
{"id":14,"explanation":"Keep these Status enum values coordinated with STATUS_LABELS in web/src/status.ts. Reordering the iota declarations changes the integer assigned to each status, causing stored integers to mean a different status. #88 is the related issue.","unsure":""}
{"id":15,"explanation":"The index must order its columns as tenant_id, then created_at, to match the orders_by_tenant query. ix_orders_tenant is the index being created; orders_by_tenant names the query it supports.","unsure":""}
{"id":16,"explanation":"TList.Add must keep FCount no greater than the length of FItems. The Grow operation is expected to preserve that capacity invariant when adding an item. FCount and FItems are the count and storage members named in the comment.","unsure":""}
{"id":17,"explanation":"Call _flush before closing _sink because closing it drops _buf. Reversing those operations could discard buffered data. The underscored names refer to the flush method, sink and buffer used by close.","unsure":""}
{"id":18,"explanation":"Treat userPath as untrusted request input: resolve it under workspaceRoot and reject paths whose .. components escape that root. The shown line resolves and normalizes the path; the comment also requires an escape check, which is not shown.","unsure":""}
{"id":19,"explanation":"Drain ring before freeing buf. Otherwise an interrupt handler could still use the freed buffer, causing a use-after-free. ring_drain and buf identify the drain operation and buffer; #2201 is the related issue.","unsure":""}
{"id":20,"explanation":"Reserve capacity for n items before inserting them to use one allocation. Earlier push_back growth accounted for 40% of the measured profile. doc:perf-notes#ingest points to the ingest section of the performance notes.","unsure":""}
{"id":21,"explanation":"Use HOLDLOCK on this SQL Server MERGE so concurrent upserts do not race. doc:mssql-merge#concurrency points to the concurrency section of the cited MERGE documentation.","unsure":""}
{"id":22,"explanation":"Use an autonomous transaction for the audit write so its row survives even if the caller rolls back its own transaction. PRAGMA AUTONOMOUS_TRANSACTION is the statement that makes that choice.","unsure":""}
{"id":23,"explanation":"To remove a constraint in SQLite, rebuild the table because SQLite ALTER TABLE cannot drop it directly. leases_new is the replacement table being created; doc:sqlite-alter#otheralter points to the relevant SQLite ALTER TABLE documentation section.","unsure":""}
{"id":24,"explanation":"The loop polls every five seconds because the NFS mounts in question do not emit inotify events. done.wait(5) supplies the interval, and scan performs each poll.","unsure":""}
{"id":25,"explanation":"Keep the for...of loop when awaiting run(job). Using an async callback inside forEach would let its errors escape the surrounding try/catch. forEach and for...of are the two iteration forms named in the warning.","unsure":""}
{"id":26,"explanation":"Every decoded Vec<u8> frame must have length at most MAX_FRAME. read_frame’s decoder rejects larger frames; MAX_FRAME is the named size limit.","unsure":""}
{"id":27,"explanation":"Register drainHook with addShutdownHook before calling pool.start(). The hook drains the pool when SIGTERM arrives, including if shutdown occurs soon after the pool starts. SIGTERM is the termination signal named in the comment.","unsure":""}
{"id":28,"explanation":"Move backoff’s fixed retry policy into configuration. This task is done when Default in internal/retry/policy.go reads that configuration; the path identifies the file and Default identifies the named symbol.","unsure":""}
{"id":29,"explanation":"See issue #412 for the full matrix of transitions among the LeaseState values Active, Released and Stale.","unsure":""}
{"id":30,"explanation":"Pass TOKEN to tool login through standard input with --token-stdin, never as a command-line argument. ps can expose command-line arguments to other users. TOKEN is the shell variable holding the secret; argv is a process’s argument list.","unsure":""}
{"id":31,"explanation":"Create the app_reader role before granting it SELECT privileges. Migration 042 assumes that role already exists; app_reader is the role name and 042 identifies the dependent migration.","unsure":""}
{"id":32,"explanation":"Make on_event idempotent because Stripe can retry a webhook for three days; processing the same event again must not duplicate its effects. doc:stripe#webhook-retries points to the webhook retries section of the cited Stripe documentation.","unsure":""}
{"id":33,"explanation":"Compare sig and expected with hmac.Equal so MAC comparison takes constant time. Using == could reveal information through timing. hmac.Equal is the required comparison function; sig and expected are the two MAC values.","unsure":""}
{"id":34,"explanation":"Build the CodeGraph from facts once per index, at a cost proportional to the number of edges. Do not rebuild it for each query. CodeGraph::build is the named construction method.","unsure":""}
{"id":35,"explanation":"Changing the layout of TCustomerRec can make existing saved .dat files unreadable or misinterpreted. If the packed record layout changes, increment FILE_VERSION, the named file-format version value.","unsure":""}
{"id":36,"explanation":"Change MSG_MAX and MSG_MAX_WIRE together because their limits are coupled. proto/messages.h is the file containing MSG_MAX_WIRE; MSG_MAX is the constant defined here.","unsure":""}
{"id":37,"explanation":"When handler runs after authMiddleware, session.userId must be non-null. authMiddleware is the named middleware responsible for establishing that condition.","unsure":""}
{"id":38,"explanation":"Keep the cache operation synchronized because checking for an entry and then inserting it must be one atomic operation. A ConcurrentHashMap alone would not make that sequence atomic; cache is the object being locked.","unsure":""}
{"id":39,"explanation":"Validate a user-provided repository alias against the registry before using it in a path join. validate_alias is the validation call, raw is the untrusted value, and #1996 is the related security issue.","unsure":""}
{"id":40,"explanation":"Each inserted payment Amount must be nonnegative. Represent refunds as separate rows with Kind equal to 'R', rather than as negative amounts. Amount and Kind are the inserted columns; @amt and @kind supply their values.","unsure":""}
{"id":41,"explanation":"Set stop_ before joining worker_. Otherwise the worker may keep running and worker_.join() may block forever. Those names identify the stop flag and worker being joined.","unsure":""}
{"id":42,"explanation":"The limiter reflects the stated GitHub API allowance of 5,000 requests per hour per token. rate.Every(720*time.Millisecond) spaces requests at roughly that rate, with a burst size of one. doc:github-rest#rate-limits points to the rate-limits documentation section.","unsure":""}
{"id":43,"explanation":"Stop the cidx service before replacing /usr/local/bin/cidx. Replacing a binary while it is running can fail with “Text file busy.” The systemctl command stops the service; the install command writes the replacement binary.","unsure":""}
{"id":44,"explanation":"Do not share _lease across jobs: doing so could expose one user’s credentials to another. _lease is the CredentialLease field beside the warning, and #77 is the related issue.","unsure":"The snippet does not show whether this field belongs to a per-job object or is actually shared across jobs."}
{"id":45,"explanation":"Keep len(ids) at or below 999 when building this DELETE statement. Each ID uses a bound variable, and the comment states that 999 is SQLite’s per-statement bound-variable limit. marks supplies the placeholders; ids supplies their values.","unsure":""}
{"id":46,"explanation":"If cgroup.create(limit) fails, log a warning and let the job continue. This deliberate degradation keeps a bad memory estimate from aborting the job; cgroup.create is the containment setup call.","unsure":""}
{"id":47,"explanation":"Remove the legacy /v1/jobs route registered here. #530 tracks that remaining work.","unsure":""}
{"id":48,"explanation":"Consult doc:adr-007#session-model for the reason SessionRegistry keeps sessions per tab. The reference points to the session-model section of ADR 007.","unsure":""}
{"id":49,"explanation":"The partial index speeds up lookup of active leases by credential_id; the stated measurement is 30 times faster. Its WHERE released_at IS NULL condition keeps released leases out of the index.","unsure":""}
{"id":50,"explanation":"Keep EVENT_TYPES coordinated with EVENT_LABELS in web/src/events.ts and with docs/events.md. The first path identifies the TypeScript file and label symbol; the second identifies the events documentation. Changes to the listed event types must be reflected in both.","unsure":""}
